---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="Search tips" description="Everything you can type in whereti: words, quotes, or and not, wildcards, file types, dates, sizes, folders, inside documents and archives, by meaning, photos by place, camera and date taken, music and documents by their details, duplicates, past searches and the keyboard." prose>
  <h1>Search tips</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Plain words are enough almost always. When you want more, everything below works in the search box, and it can be
    combined. In whereti, the <strong>?</strong> in the search box (or <kbd>F1</kbd>) shows the same list, and a click on
    an example tries it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="words">Words</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Finds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>report 2025</code></td><td>Names with every word, anywhere and in any order. Capitals and accents do not matter: <code>masina</code> finds “Mașină”.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"annual report"</code></td><td>These words together, exactly as typed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>invoice | receipt</code></td><td>One or the other.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>report !draft</code></td><td>Leaves out names with the word after <code>!</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>*.pdf</code>, <code>img_????.jpg</code></td><td>The whole name: <code>*</code> stands for any letters, <code>?</code> for exactly one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>projects\whereti</code></td><td>A backslash searches the path, not only the name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>facutra</code></td><td>A typo is forgiven: when a word of 4 letters or more finds almost nothing, names with that word one letter off show under “Close matches”, so <code>facutra</code> finds “factura”.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="filters">Filters</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Keeps</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ext:pdf;docx</code></td><td>Only these file types.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>dm:today</code></td>
          <td>
            Changed on a day or in a period: <code>today</code>, <code>yesterday</code>, <code>thisweek</code>,
            <code>lastweek</code>, <code>thismonth</code>, <code>lastmonth</code>, <code>thisyear</code>,
            <code>lastyear</code>, a year <code>2025</code>, a month <code>2025-03</code>, a day <code>2025-03-15</code>
            (or <code>15.03.2025</code>).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>dm:2025-03-01..2025-06-30</code></td>
          <td>Changed between two dates. Months and years work too: <code>dm:2025-03..2025-06</code>, <code>dm:2023..2024</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>dm:&gt;2025-06</code></td><td>Changed after (or <code>&lt;</code> before) a date.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>size:&gt;10mb</code></td>
          <td>
            By size: <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, a range
            <code>1mb..5mb</code>, or a word: <code>empty</code>, <code>tiny</code> (under 10 KB), <code>small</code>
            (to 100 KB), <code>medium</code> (to 1 MB), <code>large</code> (to 16 MB), <code>huge</code> (to 128 MB),
            <code>gigantic</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>folder:projects</code></td><td>Only folders with that name (<code>folder:</code> alone: only folders). <code>file:</code> does the same for files.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>path:C:\Users</code></td><td>Only inside a folder.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mine:</code></td><td>Only your own files, without programs, system and hidden files.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>regex:^IMG_\d{4}</code></td><td>A regular expression on the name, for experts.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="inside">Inside files and photos</h2>
  <p>
    Plain words also look inside documents: results whose text has every word come under the names, with the line where
    they appear. To search only inside, or only photos:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Finds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>content:rent</code>, <code>c:rent</code>, <code>contains:rent</code></td><td>Only files that have the words inside (PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OpenDocument, e-mails, e-books, XPS, text, and the text in scans and photos; see <a href="/faq/#formats">every format</a>). Other words in the search then filter by name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rental contract</code></td><td>With “Search documents by meaning” turned on in Settings, Search, two or more words also find documents that say the same thing in other words, under “Close in meaning”: here, the lease. Best within one language. See <a href="/faq/#meaning">Questions</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loc:italy</code></td><td>Photos taken in a town, region or country, from the GPS position your phone or camera wrote into them. <code>rome</code>, <code>roma</code> or <code>italia</code> work as plain words too.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dog</code>, <code>beach</code>, <code>car</code></td><td>Photos that show it, if you turned on “Recognize objects and animals in photos” (see <a href="/faq/#photos">Questions</a>). Any of whereti's 8 languages works: <code>câine</code>, <code>Hund</code>, <code>perro</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Archives</strong> are searched too: a ZIP, 7z or RAR file is found by the names of the files inside it, and a
    ZIP or 7z also by the text of the documents inside (a very large ZIP by its list of names only).
  </p>

  <h2 id="details">Photos, music and documents by their details</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Keeps</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>taken:2025-08</code></td><td>Photos taken on a day, in a month or a year, or in a range: <code>taken:2025-06..2025-08</code>. The date the camera wrote, not when the file was copied. Not yet for some RAW formats (CR3, RAF, ORF).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>camera:iphone</code></td><td>Photos taken with a camera or phone whose name has that word: <code>camera:canon</code>, <code>camera:pixel</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>width:&gt;4000</code>, <code>height:&lt;1000</code></td><td>Images by their size in pixels, with <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code> or an exact number.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>artist:queen</code>, <code>album:...</code></td><td>Music by its artist or album.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>duration:&gt;5m</code></td><td>Music and videos by length: <code>90s</code>, <code>5m</code>, <code>1h</code>, <code>3m30s</code>, or a range like <code>duration:1m..5m</code>. A value covers its whole last unit: <code>5m</code> is 5:00 to 5:59, so <code>&gt;5m</code> means longer than 5:59. Videos only in MP4 and its kin for now, not MKV or AVI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>author:popescu</code></td><td>Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OpenDocument files by their author (not PDF yet).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>They combine with everything else: <code>camera:canon taken:2025 loc:italia</code>.</p>
  <p>
    Photos from cameras and phones (JPG, HEIC, AVIF, JPEG XL, TIFF, RAW) are read for place and contents anywhere on your
    disks; PNG and WebP only in Pictures and the folders read for documents. The text in pictures (a sign, a receipt, a
    screenshot) is read anywhere, in every format but camera RAW. Music and videos are read anywhere too, outside your own folders
    from 1 MB up. Program, cache, hidden and system files, and files only in the cloud, are never read. The preview shows
    what whereti read from the selected file: what a photo shows, when and with which camera it was taken and its size in
    pixels, a song's artist, album and length, a document's author.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dupes">Duplicates</h2>
  <p>
    <code>dupe:</code> finds identical files across your disks, compared by their content, not only their size. The sets
    that would free the most space come first. Add any filter to narrow it: <code>dupe: size:&gt;100mb</code>,
    <code>dupe: ext:jpg</code>. A set shows only when every byte of its files matched. Very large files are compared
    over several searches, each going on where the last stopped. Hard links are not counted as copies, and files only in
    the cloud are never read.
  </p>

  <h2 id="combine">Together</h2>
  <p>
    Everything combines: <code>invoice ext:pdf dm:thisyear</code>, <code>loc:greece dm:2025-08</code>,
    <code>contract !draft path:D:\Company size:&lt;5mb</code>. The tabs above the results (Documents, Images, Audio, Video,
    Folders) add their own filter on top; Images shows the results as a grid of thumbnails.
  </p>

  <h2 id="history">Past searches</h2>
  <p>
    With the search box empty, <kbd>↑</kbd> brings back what you searched before. Past searches also show as buttons on
    the empty page; the small × on one forgets it. Settings, Search, “Remember your searches” turns this off, and Forget
    clears the list. It stays on your PC.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keys">Keyboard</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>Opens whereti from any app (you can pick another shortcut in Settings). Pressed while an app's Open or Save window is in front, <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a result takes that window to the result's folder.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd>, <kbd>Page Up</kbd> <kbd>Page Down</kbd></td><td>Move through the results. In the Images grid, the arrows move by tile and by line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> in an empty box</td><td>Brings back your past searches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Opens the file, folder, app or Windows setting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Shows the file in its folder, in Explorer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Tab</kbd></td><td>Shows or hides the preview.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>F2</kbd></td><td>Renames the file, in a box like Explorer's.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Delete</kbd></td><td>Moves the file to the Recycle Bin, when you are in the list (the box is empty, or you moved with the arrows or clicked). On a network drive, which has no Recycle Bin, whereti asks first and deletes for good.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Copies the file itself: paste it into a folder, an e-mail or a chat.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Copies the path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F10</kbd></td><td>The usual right-click menu of Explorer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>F1</kbd></td><td>The list of search commands.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>,</kbd></td><td>Settings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Clears the search; with nothing typed, hides whereti.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="order">What comes first</h2>
  <p>whereti puts the file you most likely want on top, in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>How well the name matches:</strong> the exact name (with or without its extension), then names that start with the word, then a word inside the name, then anywhere.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Then, among names that match as well:</strong> your own folders (Desktop, Documents, Downloads, Pictures…)
      before program and system folders; documents, photos, music and videos before files programs keep for
      themselves; recent changes before old ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What you open:</strong> files you open often or lately, in whereti or in other apps, move up. A file you
      opened yesterday can pass one that only starts with the word.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where you work:</strong> folders in which your own files were created or changed in the last days move up
      too. Settings, Search shows which folders whereti learnt.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The last two are learnt on your PC and never leave it. Each has a switch in Settings, Search; turning it off forgets
    what was learnt. The same file in several places shows once, with “+2 copies”.
  </p>
</Base>
